# Intro
Reproduces the D-type expansion convergence test from Hu et al. (2017,
MNRAS 471, 2151), Appendix A -- the base algorithm this branch's own HII
photoionization scheme builds on, before Smith et al. (2021)'s angular
(HEALPix) budget-splitting addition. Four star particles co-located at the
box center, each with `Q_H = 2.5e48` photons/s (`star_mass=19.2 Msun` in
this code's fit), `S_tot = 1e49` photons/s combined -- "typical for an O
star" (Hu et al. 2017). Uniform pure-hydrogen medium, `n_H = 100 cm^-3`.
No clump: this reuses `../StromgrenSphereClumpSmith2021/`'s
`makeIC_clump.py` with `clump_radius_pc=0`, which cleanly degenerates to
a uniform box (verified directly: 0 clump particles, no carve-out from
the diffuse background).

Use `../StromgrenSphereClumpSmith2021/` (with its clump intact) to assess
differences between the uniform ("spherical", `nside=0`) and angular
(`nside=1`) budget-splitting schemes -- there is no precise published
metric for that comparison, only Smith et al.'s own qualitative
mass-biasing argument. This example is the opposite: a *uniform* medium
with a precise, published analytic target (STARBENCH), specifically so
the angular-splitting question doesn't confound the validation.

## Validating against STARBENCH, not point-by-point against the papers
Hu et al. (2017) validate their own D-type test against the classical
Spitzer (1978) solution at early times and against Bisbas et al. (2015)'s
STARBENCH semi-empirical formula at late times (quoting their own
Appendix A directly: "This late-time evolution is investigated in Bisbas
et al. (2015) using several different radiative transfer codes as part of
the code comparison project STARBENCH"). `hu_smith_analytic_check.py`
does the same thing this branch's `StromgrenSphereStarbench/` example
does for a single source: numerically integrate STARBENCH's Eqns 8
(Raga-I) and 11 (Raga-II), blend via Eqns 28-29, and compare against the
simulated `r_HII(t)`.

## Temperature: Z=0, this code's own floors, not the papers' flat 1e4 K
`GEARChemistry:initial_metallicity=0` -- literally pure hydrogen,
matching Hu et al. and Smith et al.'s own medium exactly (`X_H=1`, no
metal term in either paper's algorithm). This code's own
metallicity-dependent collisional-equilibrium fit
(`radiation_get_T_collisional_K`) gives `T_i~47500 K` at `Z=0`, not the
papers' flat `T_i=1e4 K` -- see "What actually happened" below for why
`Z/Zsun~0.231` (this project's usual workaround to hit `T_i=1e4 K`
exactly) is *not* used here. The STARBENCH comparison is evaluated at
whatever `T_i`/`T_o` this run actually used
(`hu_smith_analytic_check.py --T-ionized-K --T-neutral-K`, defaulting to
47500/1000), not hardcoded to the papers' own numbers -- this remains a
valid, precise check even though it isn't a literal point-by-point
reproduction of Hu et al.'s own trajectory (their own curve was measured
at `T_i=1e4 K`; ours is a physically different, hotter, faster-expanding
region by design).

## What actually happened (2026-07-19): the neutral gas was not
## staying anywhere near `T_o`
The first version of this example used `Z/Zsun=0.231` (to hit `T_i=1e4 K`
exactly, this project's usual workaround) and left
`SPH:minimal_temperature` at its usual value of 1 K. A direct check on
the sibling `StromgrenSphereStarbench` example (same metallicity/floor
choice, denser medium) found untagged (neutral) gas sitting at **2-5 K**
from the very first snapshot onward, not anywhere near `T_o`. Real
metal-line cooling at `Z/Zsun=0.231` (absent from the papers' literal
pure-H test) combined with no floor to stop it, and the gas just kept
cooling. The same diagnostic on this example's own (accidentally
full-length, coarse-resolution) smoke test confirmed the identical
pattern at `n_H=100/cm^3` -- not just at the STARBENCH example's much
higher density, so this is a general effect of the metallicity choice,
not a density-specific one. A collapsed `T_o` collapses `c_o`
(`R_STAG = (c_i/c_o)^(4/3) R_St` -- STARBENCH Eqn 14), removing
essentially all the pressure confinement the late-phase stagnation
radius depends on.

Fix: `SPH:minimal_temperature=1000` (floors the neutral gas near `T_o`
without forcing a full isothermal EOS -- gas can still heat freely via
compression/shocks, it just can't cool below this) combined with `Z=0`
(removes the metal-line cooling that was driving the collapse, and is
the more literal reproduction of the papers' pure-H medium besides). Not
yet re-validated against a completed run at time of writing -- update
this section once it has been.

## Box size
No box size is specified in Hu et al. (2017)'s own description (it's an
SPH paper, not grid-based, so no domain size to report). At `Z/Zsun=0.231`
(`T_i=1e4 K`), `R_STAG = (c_i/c_o)^{4/3} R_St ~ 23.2 pc` (STARBENCH
Eqn 14) motivated `boxsize=0.07` kpc (70 pc, 35 pc half-width, ~1.5x
`R_STAG`). At `Z=0`'s `T_i~47500 K`, `R_STAG` grows to **~104.6 pc**
(`c_i` alone grows ~2.2x, and `R_STAG ~ c_i^{4/3}`) -- far beyond this
box. Growing the box to match would need a >20x larger particle count at
fixed resolution (mass in a uniform box scales as `L^3`), not practical
for a repository example. The box was left unchanged; the comparison
window shrinks accordingly: `R_SB(t)` stays inside the 35 pc half-width
only until `t~3.2 Myr` (vs.\ the full 8 Myr run) -- still a substantial
fraction (40%) of the run and a full order of magnitude of growth
(`R_St=5.02 pc` to `35 pc`) to compare against, the same "limited but
still meaningful" tradeoff `StromgrenSphereStarbench` and the sibling
`StromgrenSphere` example's own e10 checks already accept.

## Resolution
Hu et al. tested four gas particle masses: 4, 0.5, 0.0625, and
0.0078125 Msun (each 8x finer than the last), to establish the mass
resolution needed to converge on the analytic D-type curve. This
example's default, `gas_mass=4.0` Msun, is their own *coarsest* tested
level (~212k particles at this box size) -- chosen for a repository
example that runs in a reasonable time, not to reproduce their
highest-resolution reference. Override with e.g. `gas_mass=0.5 ./run.sh`
for a tighter test (~1.7M particles, proportionally slower); the two
finest levels (13.5M and 108M particles) are Hu et al.'s own
high-resolution convergence runs, not practical repository example
defaults.

## Gravity
`--external-gravity` is passed (matching every other example in this
family) but no `Potential:` block is configured, so there is no
gravitational force in practice -- matching Hu et al.'s and Smith et
al.'s own purely hydrodynamical test.

## Usage
```
./run.sh
python3 hu_smith_analytic_check.py -s snap/snapshot_*.hdf5
```
Override any parameter via the matching env var -- see `run.sh` for the
full list, including `gas_mass` for a finer resolution test.
